            round_done: `Year closed`,
            r1_brief: `Monday 11 January 2027. Seguros Guadalbar, in Seville, has 380,000 motor and home policies and receives about 23,000 claims a quarter, averaging €1,850. Inés Carvajal, ten years a claims adjuster and two in internal audit, takes over the claims investigation unit today: 12 investigators who can close about 1,260 full investigations a quarter. The company estimates that 5% of claims contain fraud, with a fraudulent part averaging €2,400: €2.76 million a quarter at stake. The 212 legacy rules flag 14% of claims, about 3,226 alerts a quarter, and only 22% turn out to be fraud. The rest wait with their payment frozen; the unit closed 2026 with 1,900 alerts pending. Her boss, the claims director Álvaro Mena, wants more “savings detected”; Customer Service wants the queue gone. This quarter still runs the old way: payment frozen while under investigation, and whatever is not finished moves to the next quarter. Today you decide which tool detects, at what threshold, and in what order cases are investigated.`,
            r2_brief: `Thursday 1 April. Customer Service logs a complaint a day from customers who “did nothing and are treated like criminals”, and brokers say Guadalbar “pays late and suspects everyone”. The insurance contract law requires the insurer to pay at least the undisputed amount within 40 days and penalises unjustified delay with interest. Today you decide how a flagged customer is treated, what happens to alerts not finished by quarter end, and what the tool learns from each closed case. Freezing payment costs about €150 per honest customer investigated (complaints, interest, policies not renewed); paying the undisputed part within 40 days, with a named contact and a 30-day deadline, costs about €30, but each case takes 8% more time. Refusing an honest claim without evidence costs about €1,800 between complaint, lawsuit, interest and a lost customer.`,
            r3_brief: `Monday 5 July. The data analyst cross-matches claims, body shops, clinics and bank accounts and finds a ring in the Aljarafe: 3 body shops and 2 physiotherapy clinics generating whiplash claims worth about €300,000 of fraud a quarter. There are 240 pending claims linked to them, and about 55% are genuine accident victims treated there in good faith. Inés also has to decide which deterrent starts in October and how the unit will measure its results for the committee: the 2026 report counted only “savings detected”, the claims refused.`,
            r4_brief: `Monday 4 October. The committee approves the 2028 budget and asks what the unit should look like. Inés decides how capacity grows, how the team’s bonus is paid and at what threshold the tool runs in the last quarter, with what the year has taught her. This quarter’s result includes the fraud no longer filed thanks to the deterrent chosen in July and whatever is recovered from the ring.`,
            brief_done: `The year is over. Review the net result, the fraud prevented, the honest customers affected and any refusals without evidence in the final summary.`,
